# Public storefront — shoppers, mostly signed out
#
# Copy to .env.local for development. `.env.local` is gitignored; this template
# is tracked on purpose so a fresh clone can see the contract.
#
# Every variable here is validated by src/lib/env.schema.ts, which next.config.ts
# runs at BUILD time. A missing or malformed value fails `next build` with one
# line per problem rather than throwing on some later request — so if the build
# is complaining about the environment, this file is the thing to fix.

# Origin of rimalis-api. NO trailing slash, no path.
# Local: the API defaults to port 4000 (see rimalis-api/.env.example).
#
# ⚠️ The NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix is inherited from the Part B scaffold and is
# MISLEADING. Under the BFF (ADR-0003) the browser never calls this origin: it
# calls this app's own route handlers and Server Actions, which call the API
# server-to-server. A browser-side fetch would even appear to work locally,
# because the API's dev `corsOrigins` allows localhost:5173 — and then fail
# completely in production, where `corsOrigins` is `[]`. `src/lib/env.ts` is
# `server-only` specifically so that mistake is a build error.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:4000

# Path prefix every API route mounts under. MUST match BASE_PATH on the API —
# it is a variable rather than a constant so the two cannot drift.
API_BASE_PATH=/api/v1

# This app's own public origin, no trailing slash. Two uses, both of which break
# in ways that are hard to trace if it is wrong:
#
#   1. The Paystack `callbackUrl` — get this wrong and a shopper who has just
#      been charged lands on the wrong domain.
#   2. Absolute URLs in OpenGraph metadata and the sitemap.
#
# It cannot be derived from the incoming request: a Worker behind a proxy sees
# whatever `Host` the proxy chose to send.
#
# ⚠️ Must match `APP_URL` on the rimalis-api side. The API hardcodes
# `/store/:slug`, `/orders/:id` and `/verify-email?token=` off its own APP_URL
# when it builds transactional emails, so if the two disagree every emailed link
# points at the wrong app — or at localhost, which is APP_URL's default.
APP_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Session
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Under the BFF (ADR-0003) this app's route handlers hold the session cookie, so
# the secret that encrypts it belongs here, NOT on the API.
#
# The cookie is ENCRYPTED, not merely signed — JWE `dir` + A256GCM via `jose`.
# `httpOnly` stops JavaScript reading it; it does not stop a TLS-terminating
# proxy log, a browser profile on disk, or a `Set-Cookie` echoed into an
# observability trace (and wrangler.jsonc has `observability.enabled`). So the
# access and refresh tokens inside it stay unreadable rather than merely
# untampered-with.
#
# A256GCM needs exactly 32 bytes. Generate one — do not invent a passphrase,
# which will fail the byte-length check:
#
#     openssl rand -base64url 32
#
# ...or, on an openssl too old for -base64url:
#
#     openssl rand -base64 32 | tr '+/' '-_' | tr -d '='
#
# Use a DIFFERENT secret per environment. Rotating it invalidates every session,
# which logs everyone out but is otherwise safe — the cart is a separate cookie
# and survives.
#
# ⚠️ When the cookie is set, scope it to THIS EXACT SUBDOMAIN (shop.example.com)
# and never to `.example.com`. A parent-domain cookie is readable by all three
# apps and silently destroys the session separation that three subdomains exist
# to provide. The implementation omits the `Domain` attribute entirely, which is
# strictly safer than writing the right host — it cannot be mistyped into a
# leading dot.
SESSION_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Development escape hatches
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Serve the catalogue from local fixtures instead of the live API. Set to 1 only
# if the API is genuinely unreachable — public reads only, never auth, orders or
# payments, because a mocked checkout hides every bug worth finding. A banner
# renders while it is on, and the branch is dead-code-eliminated in a production
# build (see the NODE_ENV guard in src/lib/env.ts).
#
# The API's seed covers 16 products across 5 vendors with deliberate edge cases,
# so this should stay off. It is a fallback, not a plan, and Phase 8 deletes it.
# MARKETPLACE_FIXTURES=1
